Take $X=\mathbb{R}^2$, the subspace $M$ the $x$-axis, and $f(t,0)=t$ on $M$. With any of the norms $\|\cdot\|_1,\|\cdot\|_2,\|\cdot\|_\infty$ we have $|f(t,0)|=\|(t,0)\|$, so $\|f\|=1$ on $M$. The linear extensions are $F_c(x,y)=x+cy$, and the question is which have norm $1$ on all of $X$.

The norm of $F_c$ is the dual norm of the vector $(1,c)$, which is $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ for $(\mathbb{R}^2,\|\cdot\|_1)$, $\|\cdot\|_1$ for $(\mathbb{R}^2,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ and $\|\cdot\|_2$ for the Euclidean norm. So:

- for $\|\cdot\|_1$, $\|F_c\|=\max(1,|c|)$, and every $c\in[-1,1]$ works;
- for $\|\cdot\|_\infty$, $\|F_c\|=1+|c|$, and only $c=0$ works;
- for $\|\cdot\|_2$, $\|F_c\|=\sqrt{1+c^2}$, and only $c=0$ works.

Geometrically, a norm-one extension is a line $F_c=1$ touching the unit ball at $(1,0)$ without cutting into it. The diamond of $\|\cdot\|_1$ has a corner at $(1,0)$, so there are many such supporting lines; the square and the disc have only one. Extensions always exist here, but need not be unique. The theorem says that existence survives in every dimension.

Let $T$ be a compact operator on a Hilbert space $H$, let $\lambda\ne0$ and $A=T-\lambda I$. Then:

1. $\ker A$ is finite-dimensional;
2. the range $A(H)$ is closed, and $A(H)=\ker(A^*)^\perp$ where $A^*=T^*-\bar\lambda I$;
3. $\ker A=\{0\}$ if and only if $A(H)=H$;
4. $\dim\ker A=\dim\ker A^*$ (stated without proof; the proof below covers parts 1–3).

In words: either $Tu-\lambda u=f$ has a unique solution for every $f$, or the homogeneous equation $Tu=\lambda u$ has finitely many independent solutions, and then $Tu-\lambda u=f$ is solvable exactly when $f$ is orthogonal to every solution of $T^*v=\bar\lambda v$. The same theorem holds for compact operators on Banach spaces (Riesz–Schauder theory), with annihilators in the dual space in place of orthogonal complements.

Here is a made-up joint distribution of $X\in\{0,1,2\}$ and $Y\in\{0,1\}$:

- $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.1$, $P(X=0,Y=1)=0.2$;
- $P(X=1,Y=0)=0.2$, $P(X=1,Y=1)=0.1$;
- $P(X=2,Y=0)=0.3$, $P(X=2,Y=1)=0.1$.

Row sums give the marginal of $X$: $P(X=0)=0.3$, $P(X=1)=0.3$, $P(X=2)=0.4$. Column sums give $P(Y=0)=0.6$, $P(Y=1)=0.4$. Then $E[X]=0.3+0.8=1.1$ and $E[Y]=0.4$.

$X$ and $Y$ are not independent, because $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.1$ while $P(X=0)P(Y=0)=0.18$. One failing cell is enough. Conditioning on $Y$ changes the distribution of $X$: given $Y=0$, the probabilities of $X=0,1,2$ are $\frac{0.1}{0.6},\frac{0.2}{0.6},\frac{0.3}{0.6}$, that is $\frac16,\frac13,\frac12$, so $E[X\mid Y=0]=\frac13+1=\frac43$; given $Y=1$ they are $\frac12,\frac14,\frac14$, so $E[X\mid Y=1]=\frac14+\frac12=\frac34$. Averaging these with weights $P(Y=0)=0.6$ and $P(Y=1)=0.4$ gives $0.8+0.3=1.1=E[X]$. This is the tower property, proved below.

